    pr_ref:  # ac28
    description: "Bake the generative question 'what question did we forget to ask?' into the four natural inflection points where it is the right question to ask: per-release, post-incident, pre-refactor, and domain-shift moments. Complementary to the per-commit cadence hook (.agent/hooks/_shared/check_audit_cadence.py) which fires on a commit-count tempo that systematically misses these inflection points, and even when it fires the agent still has to pick a suspect — these bake-ins make sure the question gets asked at the moments where it's most productive.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nThe Fundamental Concept Audit playbook (.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/what-if-we-dont-know-what-the-fuck-we-are-talking-about-audit-aka-fundamental-concept-audit.md) is purely DIAGNOSTIC — given a one-sentence suspicion, it tells you how to investigate. It does not help when nobody suspects anything. The cadence hook (PR #3460) surfaces a 'time to audit' reminder every ~72 development commits, but the agent still has to pick a suspect domain — and that requires asking 'what question have we not asked yet?', which is a different mode (generative, not diagnostic).\n\nA purely commit-count cadence misses the four natural moments where the generative question is most productive:\n- *Per-release boundary*: you are already pausing to tag and ship; cheap to spend 5 minutes asking 'what would surprise me about this release in retrospect?'\n- *Post-incident*: an incident IS evidence of an unasked question. Without an explicit step, the lesson stays narrow ('fix this specific bug') instead of widening ('what other unasked questions of this shape exist?').\n- *Pre-refactor*: a major structural change locks in a frame. If a frame-level question is going to be missed, this is the last cheap moment to ask it.\n- *Domain-shift*: new language analyzer, new framework support, new contributor with fresh eyes. The newness is the asset; spend it before assimilating into the existing frame.\n\nDELIVERABLES (each independently shippable; ship at least 2 of the 4):\n\n1. *Per-release bake-in.* scripts/prepare-release adds a step before the version-bump that prints (or interactively prompts):\n   '5-minute check: what question might we have forgotten to ask about this release? Skim docs/blind-spots.md (if it exists, else just take 5 minutes to consider frame-level assumptions). Continue when ready.'\n   The release-workflow playbook (.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/release-workflow.md) gets the same prompt added to its 'Agent Preparation' section.\n\n2. *Post-incident bake-in.* When an incident retrospective is written — whether via the existing agentic-session-retrospective playbook (.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/agentic-session-retrospective.md) or a new incident-retro playbook — add a final step:\n   'Decide whether the unasked question that produced this incident belongs as a new entry in docs/blind-spots.md (sibling item WI-mivir-motom-mopun-hukod-bobuv-mabob-tinil-zidin captures the doc itself). If the doc does not yet exist, log the candidate shape in the retrospective itself so it can be promoted later.'\n\n3. *Pre-refactor bake-in.* The structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol playbook (.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol.md) — or a new pre-refactor playbook if scoped separately — adds a 'Skim blind spots' step before declaring the new frame: 'Before locking in a structural change, spend 5 minutes considering what frame-level question this refactor might foreclose.'\n\n4. *Domain-shift bake-in.* When a new language analyzer or framework support lands (or a new contributor onboards), the PR description prompt — or an analyzer-onboarding playbook — adds a question:\n   'Did anything about this new domain surprise you? What did the existing frame *almost* assume that the new domain does not satisfy?'\n   This catches frame-level questions the existing analyzer authors could not have asked because they did not yet have the contrast.\n\nEach bake-in includes a fallback for the case where docs/blind-spots.md does not yet exist (a bare '5 minutes to think' prompt still surfaces the question), so this item can ship independently of WI-mivir-motom.\n\nRELATIONSHIP TO SIBLINGS:\n- WI-mivir-motom-mopun-hukod-bobuv-mabob-tinil-zidin — docs/blind-spots.md, the catalog of question-SHAPES that the bake-in prompts skim. WI-mivir-motom originally listed these bake-ins as Deliverable B; this item supersedes that scope so bake-in work can be tracked independently of doc creation (the bake-ins are useful even without the doc; the doc is useful even without all four bake-ins).\n- WI-bofub-tudik-vajig-hadib-jodin-gosig-raniz-rudoj — concept-axes registry (done, PR #3463). The 'what' that one specific class of audit nominates from.\n- WI-gusam-faful-kamum-lujon-bumip-liral-dukag-mofip — per-release axis audit cadence. A NARROWER instance of this item's deliverable 1 (specifically about axes, not about generative questions in general). May coalesce with this item when both land.\n- WI-tavas-voror-rakiv-litog-gatad-zonoz-holah-tugil — triage 45 emitted-but-unregistered edge_type values. The kind of accumulated drift these bake-ins (especially per-release) would have caught earlier had they been in place.\n\nACCEPTANCE: at least 2 of the 4 bake-ins land with concrete prompts wired into the appropriate scripts and playbooks. Each landed bake-in survives without docs/blind-spots.md (degrades gracefully). Re-running prepare-release / drafting an incident retro / proposing a refactor / landing a new analyzer surfaces the prompt at the right moment without manual recall.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026) as loose-end #14 — the per-commit cadence hook landed (PR #3460) but the trigger-based bake-ins did not. Filed separately so the bake-ins can ship without waiting on the question-shapes catalog (WI-mivir-motom)."  # ac28
    fields: {}  # ac28
- op: discuss  # 2146
